    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct mechanistic evidence linking CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation**\n\n*CFTR loss creates a dysbiotic gut ecosystem* – Whole‑body Cftr‑KO mice develop a marked shift in fecal microbiota (↑Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcus, Proteobacteria) together with increased intestinal permeability and mucosal inflammation; gut‑corrected (CFTR‑KO‑GC) mice retain a normal microbiota and no pathology【PMID:38554847】 (animal).  Intestinal‑specific Cftr deletion (iCftr‑KO) reproduces this phenotype: beta‑diversity changes, enrichment of Proteobacteria and pathogenic Clostridia, and a rise in fecal calprotectin despite two different anti‑obstructive diets【PMID:37546931】【PMID:38073491】 (animal).  Human systematic review of 38 studies confirms reduced diversity and a pro‑inflammatory taxonomic profile in people with CF (pwCF) compared with controls【PMID:36600119】 (human/animal/in‑vitro).\n\n*Dysbiosis drives local intestinal inflammation* – In iCftr‑KO mice, the magnitude of Proteobacteria/Enterobacteriaceae expansion correlates with elevated fecal calprotectin, a quantitative marker of intestinal inflammation【PMID:37546931】 (animal).  Antibiotic reduction of the dysbiotic load restores barrier integrity and lowers hepatic inflammation in Cftr‑KO mice, indicating that the dysbiotic community is necessary for the inflammatory response【PMID:38554847】 (animal).  In vitro, CFTR‑deficient Caco‑2 cells (CFTR‑/‑) show a suppressed IL‑1β response when exposed to propionate produced by Bacteroides; removal of propionate restores the pro‑inflammatory phenotype, linking a dysbiosis‑derived metabolite to epithelial inflammation【PMID:38179971】 (in‑vitro).\n\n*Intestinal dysbiosis/inflammation propagates systemic inflammation* – Cftr‑KO mice develop peri‑portal liver inflammation and ductular proliferation; these lesions are absent in gut‑corrected mice, demonstrating that defective intestinal CFTR and its associated dysbiosis are required for extra‑intestinal (liver) inflammation【PMID:38554847】 (animal).  Human data show that ivacaftor‑mediated CFTR potentiation decreases stool calprotectin and simultaneously enriches anti‑inflammatory taxa (Akkermansia) while reducing Enterobacteriaceae, implicating a chain from restored CFTR function → microbiota normalization → reduced intestinal inflammation【PMID:30546102】 (human).  Reviews note bidirectional links between dysbiosis and low‑grade intestinal inflammation in CF, and propose that these processes may influence lung outcomes via the gut‑lung axis【PMID:35160099】【PMID:40824057】 (human/animal).\n\n**Associations (non‑causal) and testable hypotheses**\n\n- Several human studies report a correlation between dysbiosis severity and systemic markers (e.g., CRP, pulmonary function), but causality remains unproven【PMID:36600119】 (human).  \n- The gut‑liver axis model predicts that transplanting a dysbiotic microbiota into a CFTR‑competent host will not induce liver disease unless intestinal CFTR is also deficient; this was supported by the failure of fecal transfer to cause pathology"
  },
